# DFS Search in a Tree (Java) 🌲

Here's the same DFS implementation in Java for a binary tree:

```java
import java.util.*;

class TreeNode {
    int val;
    TreeNode left;
    TreeNode right;

    TreeNode(int val) {
        this.val = val;
    }
}

public class DFSInTree {

    // Pre-order DFS: Root -> Left -> Right
    public List<Integer> preorderDFS(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) return result;

        result.add(root.val);
        result.addAll(preorderDFS(root.left));
        result.addAll(preorderDFS(root.right));
        return result;
    }

    // In-order DFS: Left -> Root -> Right
    public List<Integer> inorderDFS(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) return result;

        result.addAll(inorderDFS(root.left));
        result.add(root.val);
        result.addAll(inorderDFS(root.right));
        return result;
    }

    // Post-order DFS: Left -> Right -> Root
    public List<Integer> postorderDFS(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) return result;

        result.addAll(postorderDFS(root.left));
        result.addAll(postorderDFS(root.right));
        result.add(root.val);
        return result;
    }

    // Iterative DFS (Pre-order) using Stack
    public List<Integer> dfsIterative(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) return result;

        Stack<TreeNode> stack = new Stack<>();
        stack.push(root);

        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode node = stack.pop();
            result.add(node.val);

            // Push right first so left is processed first
            if (node.right != null) stack.push(node.right);
            if (node.left != null) stack.push(node.left);
        }

        return result;
    }

    // Example usage
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create sample tree:
        //       1
        //      / \
        //     2   3
        //    / \
        //   4   5
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(1);
        root.left = new TreeNode(2);
        root.right = new TreeNode(3);
        root.left.left = new TreeNode(4);
        root.left.right = new TreeNode(5);

        DFSInTree dfs = new DFSInTree();

        System.out.println("Pre-order: " + dfs.preorderDFS(root));    // [1, 2, 4, 5, 3]
        System.out.println("In-order: " + dfs.inorderDFS(root));     // [4, 2, 5, 1, 3]
        System.out.println("Post-order: " + dfs.postorderDFS(root)); // [4, 5, 2, 3, 1]
        System.out.println("Iterative DFS: " + dfs.dfsIterative(root)); // [1, 2, 4, 5, 3]
    }
}
```

Key differences from Python:
- Explicit type declarations (`TreeNode`, `int`, `List<Integer>`)
- Using `Stack<TreeNode>` for iterative approach
- Java's `ArrayList` and its methods (`add`, `addAll`)
- Explicit class structure and `main` method

